.TH DEOPJUFY-FORMATS 7 "2026-08-21" "deopjufy 0.6.0" "Miscellaneous Information"
.SH NAME
deopjufy-formats \- OPJ and OPJU parser coverage, evidence terms, and limitations
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B deopjufy
provides partial native recovery for Origin OPJ and OPJU project files. Support
claims apply only to confirmed layouts exercised by legal synthetic fixtures or
samples tied to public records. They are not promises of complete Origin
compatibility.
.PP
There is one native parser path. No runtime backend switching, Origin runtime,
Origin Viewer, Wine, R, liborigin, remote service, telemetry, or hidden network
fallback is used.
.SH EVIDENCE TERMS
.TP
.B parser_status=ok
A parser path ran and produced evidence. This is not a whole-file grade.
.TP
.B support_class=parser
Parser-backed evidence exists.
.TP
.B support_class=partial
At least one relevant gap remains.
.TP
.B status=extracted
An artifact was written.
.TP
.B heuristic=true
The item depends on scanning, carving, proximity, tokens, or inferred bounds.
.TP
.B verification=exact
The emitted bytes or values satisfy one bounded parser contract. It does not
imply complete project parity.
.SH SUPPORT MATRIX
.TS
tab(|) box;
l l l.
Family|OPJ|OPJU
Detection and inspection|partial|partial
Versioned catalog and lazy get|partial|partial
Project tree|partial|partial
Worksheets and metadata|partial|partial
Matrices|partial|partial
Notes and functions|partial|partial
Analysis equations and bounded fields|partial|partial
Semantic provenance relationships|unsupported|partial
Report-reference tables and state|unsupported|partial
Persistent graph source bindings|partial|partial
Graph metadata and stored previews|partial|partial
Embedded images and attachments|partial|partial
Unknown-region preservation|partial|partial
Scripts|unsupported|unsupported
Whole-project parity|unsupported|unsupported
.TE
.PP
In this table, partial means at least one bounded native rule exists. It does not
mean every version, value, field, relationship, or object in that family is
decoded.
.SH OPJ
Confirmed legacy layouts can expose typed datasets, workbook and sheet ownership,
worksheet metadata, matrix shape, notes, functions, project-tree records,
attachments, and selected graph structure.
.PP
Coverage remains incomplete for version variants, masks, scripts, uncommon
attachment records, graph styling, stored-preview ownership, and complete
unknown-byte classification.
.SH OPJU
Confirmed layouts can expose selected framed or compressed regions,
OriginStorage records, media, attachments, reports, descriptor-owned worksheets,
typed column payloads, bounded metadata, decoded strings, numeric runs, and raw
source regions.
.PP
Supported relationships include bounded descriptor columns, selected
post-payload calculation references, recovered functions, report-cell paths,
recognized report-grid state fields, uniquely resolvable MSer report-reference
tables, exact analysis-report XML, and persistent graph-layer source slots.
Dataset and graph owners remain candidates unless exact evidence resolves one.
.PP
OPJU discovery is still partly scan-anchored. The parser does not implement the
complete container grammar, object graph, or every worksheet, matrix, function,
graph, report, and metadata encoding.
.SH SEMANTIC ALIASES
Structural wire labels remain present when a conservative role alias is emitted.
Version 0.6 exposes qualified aliases for confirmed project page and folder
directory entries, exact MSer string property sets, calculation-reference arrays
and members, persistent data-plot style prototype source bindings, and fully
resolved analysis-report placeholder-reference tables.
.PP
Semantic confidence is separate from byte verification. These aliases do not
promote unresolved folder membership, child-window types, recalculation states,
graph ownership, neutral flags, sentinel business types, or affine-tail
hypotheses.
.SH GRAPHS AND PREVIEWS
Graph definitions and stored previews have independent status. A preview may be
present while graph-definition parsing remains partial. A parser-bounded absence
is recorded as an exact absence and does not make the graph unsupported.
.PP
The optional viewer displays only recovered images or an exact parser-linked
stored preview. It does not render a graph definition or infer missing styling.
.SH HUMAN AND MACHINE RECOVERY
The default human profile favors content-bearing primary artifacts. Machine
profiles retain parser indexes, source maps, decoded payload evidence, and raw
unknown regions. The map profile additionally partitions every source byte into
a reconstructable byte map.
.PP
Preserving every byte and classifying every byte semantically are different
goals. Unknown regions remain explicit even when exact reconstruction succeeds.
.SH NON-CLAIMS
The project does not claim:
.IP \[bu] 2
full OPJ or OPJU support;
.IP \[bu]
whole-project or whole-application parity;
.IP \[bu]
complete version coverage;
.IP \[bu]
Origin graph rendering or project editing;
.IP \[bu]
script execution;
.IP \[bu]
that one parser-backed item proves every related object was found;
.IP \[bu]
that heuristic output is confirmed structure.
.SH CACHE AND SIDE EFFECTS
Parser and scan caches are bounded and process-local. They key file-derived work
by resolved path, size, modification time, and relevant options. Cache entries
do not persist after process exit and must not suppress warnings or alter output
order.
.PP
Inputs are expected to remain unchanged for one command. The CLI writes only to
explicit output paths, creates no application state directory, and performs no
network access during parsing or extraction.
.SH FIXTURE POLICY
Tracked Origin fixtures must be small, deterministic, author-generated,
redistributable, and registered. Real-world public samples are downloaded by an
explicit development command, verified by checksum, and remain untracked.
Unknown-license, confidential, and private projects must not enter source,
documentation, tests, manifests, screenshots, or history.
